<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Neustrelitz</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Neustrelitz is a town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecklenburg-vorpommern">Mecklenburg-Western Pomerania</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, founded in 1733 by Duke Adolphus Frederick III as the new residence of the dukes of Mecklenburg-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strelitz">Strelitz</a>. Laid out as a baroque planned town with streets radiating from a central market square, it remained the capital of Mecklenburg-Strelitz until the state merged with Mecklenburg-Schwerin in 1934.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/neustrelitz.jpg" alt="Neustrelitz" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Harke · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Marktplatz_Neustrelitz.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Neustrelitz lies on the shore of the Zierker See in the Mecklenburg Lake District, in the Mecklenburgische Seenplatte district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mecklenburg-vorpommern">Mecklenburg-Vorpommern</a>. Its name, New Strelitz, refers to the older town of Strelitz, whose own name comes from the Polabian word Strelci, meaning <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archery">archers</a> or shooters. From 1994 until 2011 Neustrelitz was the capital of the district of Mecklenburg-Strelitz.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The village of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strelitz">Strelitz</a> was first mentioned in 1278 and grew into a small town. In the 17th century it belonged to the duchy of Mecklenburg-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustrow">Güstrow</a>, which ceased to exist after the last duke died in 1695. The Duchy of Mecklenburg-Strelitz was then established in 1701, with a territory that included an exclave around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ratzeburg">Ratzeburg</a>, now in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schleswig-holstein">Schleswig-Holstein</a>. In 1712 the castle and the town of Strelitz burnt down. The duke and his family afterwards lived at their hunting lodge on the Zierker See, and the new town of Neustrelitz was built around it.</p><p>Neustrelitz remained the ducal seat until 1918 and was then the capital of the Free State of Mecklenburg-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strelitz">Strelitz</a>. In 1934 that state was merged with Mecklenburg-Schwerin to form the Gau of Mecklenburg. The old town of Strelitz survived the fire as a small village and was absorbed into Neustrelitz in 1931. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> troops of the 2nd Belorussian Front entered the town on 30 April 1945, 681 people committed suicide. During the Nazi period the town's asylum for the mentally ill took part in the mass murder of disabled people and served as a transit station for those judged unfit.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The centre is characterised by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a> architecture and centres on the Marktplatz, where the Stadtkirche, built from 1768 to 1778, faces the Rathaus of 1841 by Friedrich W. Buttel, a disciple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-friedrich-schinkel">Karl Friedrich Schinkel</a>. The Baroque palace was destroyed in 1945, but the palace gardens remain, with an 18th-century Orangerie, the neo-Gothic Schloßkirche of 1855 to 1859 and a temple of Hebe. The Louise Temple, built in 1891 in the form of a Greek temple, was meant to house the tomb of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louise-of-mecklenburg-strelitz">Queen Louise of Prussia</a>, born a princess of Mecklenburg-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strelitz">Strelitz</a>. The town has a station on the Berlin Northern Railway with direct connections to Berlin and Rostock.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Neustrelitz” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After Strelitz palace burned in 1712, Adolf Frederick III founded the planned town of <b>Neustrelitz</b> in 1733 as the new residence.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-mecklenburg-strelitz">Duchy of Mecklenburg-Strelitz</a></li><li><blockquote>The free state was a parliamentary republic with a Landtag elected by universal suffrage, governed from <b>Neustrelitz</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/free-state-of-mecklenburg-strelitz">Free State of Mecklenburg-Strelitz</a></li></ul></section>
